## Explanation
The oracle is supposed to implement the addition of a classical input $c$ and a quantum input $k$ using addition modulo $2^n$.
We will use the phase kickback trick to get the addition on the second qubit in a reversible manner.
The addition on the first $n$ qubits is achieved with a reversible circuit.
The oracle is given as follows:
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/andrey-shavrin/cs2quantumjs@main/index.js"></script>

<script type="text/javascript">
let oracle = new Oracled({
    target: 0, 
    circuit: function (qc) {
        // phase kickback
        qc.ccx(1, 0, 0);
        qc.x(0);
        qc.ccx(1, 0, 0);
        qc.x(0);

        // addition
        qc.ccx(0, 0, 1);
        qc.ccx(0, 1, 0);
        qc.t(0);
        qc.ccx(0, 1, 0);
        qc.ccx(0, 0, 1);
    }
});
oracle.addRegisters(3, [3]);
</script>

<script>
let state = new State({registers: [0b100, 0]});
let circuit = new Circuit();
oracle.apply(state, circuit);
console.log(state);
circuit.compile();
</script>

We can see that this circuit correctly implements the desired transformation on the first two qubits of the state.

